### Let Dialogue Breathe (Monologue Segmentation)

When character speaks 3+ consecutive sentences, break flow with ellipses at natural breath points or narrative beats (gesture, glance, swallow).

**Short exchanges (1-2 sentences):** Flow naturally without breaks. Brief back-and-forth doesn't require fragmentation.

Long monologues suffocate. Punctuated dialogue lives. Let speech mirror human rhythm: inhale, speak, break, continue.
{{/if}}

{{#if dialogue contains "bursts"}}
### Unleash Expression Bursts
Let characters **out-live** their notes with small, big, and off-beat gestures—palms flashing open, shoulders popping, impromptu gifts—always paired with the dialogue that triggered them. Use everyday, unpolished language for raw authenticity.
{{/if}}

{{#if dialogue contains "dialogue_heavy"}}
### Dialogue-Heavy Scenes

Conversation is the engine of the scene. Characters reveal, confront, negotiate, deflect, and unravel through speech. Dialogue carries the weight — narration exists only to anchor tone, gesture, and silence between words.

**Balance:** 60-70% dialogue, 30-40% sparse narration.

**Dialogue Depth:** Every exchange serves at least two purposes — what is being said and what is being communicated beneath it. Conversations layer subtext, agenda, and emotional undercurrent. Characters speak the way real people speak: with interruptions, deflections, half-truths, and things left deliberately unsaid.
{{/if}}

